*) → diagonal down-left: sandwich G6? No — from H5 to F7 passes through G6, which is @. Direction is NW: H5 to G6 to F7. Since F7 is *, and H5 is *, then G6 (@) is sandwiched. Yes! So both G5 (vertical) and G6 (diagonal) can be flipped.  \nThus, (H,5) flips G5 and G6 — two pieces.  \n\nNow re-check (F,1):  \nF1 is empty. Above: E1 is @, D1 is @, C1 is @, B1 is *, A1 is .. Below: G1 is @, H1 is ..  \nTo flip, need * on opposite side. From F1 up: E1(@), D1(@), C1(@), B1(*) — so F1(*), E1(@)...B1(*) → that's a sandwich along column 1. So placing * at F1 would flip E1, D1, C1 — three pieces! So (F,1) is valid and powerful.  \nEarlier mistake: assumed no connection, but actually B1 is *, so column 1 from B1 to F1: if F1 is played, it sandwiches C1, D1, E1 (@) between B1(*) and F1(*). So (F,1) flips three pieces — very strong.\n\nSo corrected evaluation:\n- (A,4): flips A3 only → 1 flip\n- (F,1): flips C1, D1, E1 → 3 flips\n- (G,2): flips G1 → 1 flip\n- (H,5): flips G5 and G6 → 2 flips\n\nBut also consider future mobility and edge control:\n- (F,1) solidifies the entire left edge (column 1), connecting A1 to H1 almost fully (only A1 and H1 missing), giving long-term stability.\n- (H,5) weakens the lower-right area but gains central control; however, H6 remains @, which might allow counterplay.\n- (A,4) is somewhat isolated and doesn’t contribute much to edge ownership.\n- (G,2) gains little and leaves G1 flipped but doesn’t open major opportunities.\n\nReason: (F,1) offers the highest immediate gain (3 flipped pieces) and secures a critical portion of the left edge, reducing opponent mobility and strengthening my frame. Though (H,5) is decent, (F,1) provides greater strategic value and material gain.\n\nAnswer: (F,1)"}
